# Frontend Project Rules (Next.js)

## Core Philosophy

- This project is frontend-first.
- The frontend owns the entire user experience.
- Backend acts only as a Session Engine and business API.
- Never move UI logic into the backend unless absolutely necessary.

## Product Mindset

Always think in terms of "Photo Experience", not pages.

Flow:
Experience
→ Session
→ Capture
→ Process
→ Output

Every feature should support this flow.

## Responsibilities

Frontend is responsible for:

- Camera access
- Live preview
- Countdown
- Frame rendering
- Capture flow
- Upload progress
- Session state
- Animations
- Responsive interaction

Backend is responsible for:

- Authentication
- Session lifecycle
- Gallery
- Storage
- Payments
- Analytics

## UX Priority

Prioritize:

1. Fast interaction
2. Smooth animation
3. Minimal waiting
4. Mobile-first
5. Touch-friendly components
6. Offline-ready architecture whenever possible

## Component Design

- Build reusable UI components.
- Keep business logic outside UI components.
- Prefer composition over large components.
- Avoid duplicated UI.
- One component should have one responsibility.

## State Management

Separate:

- UI state
- Session state
- Server state

Never mix them together.

## File Structure

Organize by feature instead of file type.

Example:

features/
session/
capture/
gallery/
experience/
auth/

Shared resources belong in:

components/
hooks/
lib/
services/
types/

## Styling

- Use Tailwind CSS.
- Prefer design tokens over hardcoded values.
- Keep spacing, radius, shadows and colors consistent.
- Avoid inline styles unless necessary.

## Performance

- Lazy load heavy features.
- Optimize images.
- Minimize rerenders.
- Avoid unnecessary client components.
- Prefer Server Components unless browser APIs are required.

## Code Quality

- Keep functions small.
- Prefer readability over cleverness.
- Avoid premature abstraction.
- Remove dead code immediately.
- Reuse existing utilities before creating new ones.

## Future Compatibility

Every implementation should make it easy to support:

- Offline Booth
- Online Booth
- Remote Group Booth
- AI Experiences

Never hardcode logic for a single experience.
Always build features to be experience-driven.
